  "Question": "When the number \\(2^{1000}\\) is divided by \\(13,\\) the remainder in the division is:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(1\\)",
    "B": "\\(2\\)",
    "C": "\\(3\\)",
    "D": "\\(7\\)",
    "E": "\\(11\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Solution": "A useful small power is \\(2^6=64=5\\cdot13-1\\), so\n\\[\n2^6\\equiv-1\\pmod{13}.\n\\]\nHere congruence modulo \\(13\\) means that the two numbers differ by a multiple of \\(13\\). Replacing factors by congruent numbers preserves the remainder of their product.\n\nSince \\(1000=6\\cdot166+4\\), we obtain\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n2^{1000}\n&=(2^6)^{166}2^4\\\\\n&\\equiv(-1)^{166}\\cdot16\\\\\n&=16\\equiv3\\pmod{13}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nBecause \\(0\\le3<13\\), the remainder is \\(\\boxed{3}\\), choice \\(\\text{C}\\).",
